welcome to the NutritionFacts family.

00:00:28.983 --> 00:00:35.117
"Is MSG Bad or Good for You?"

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.440
Reducing sodium intake
by 30 percent

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.880
could save the lives
of more than 40 million people.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.040
And guess what can reduce
sodium intake

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.400
by more than 30 percent
in standard recipes?

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.000
Substituting traditional salt
with monosodium glutamate,

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.120
commonly known as MSG.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.440
But what about "Chinese
Restaurant Syndrome,"

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.200
reported in a 1968 letter

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.360
to the New England
Journal of Medicine,

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:01.920
characterized by numbness
on the back of the neck,

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.480
general weakness,
and palpitation?

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.720
One of the proposed causes
was MSG,

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.040
a common seasoning often used
in Chinese cooking.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.040
Since then, MSG has been blamed

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:14.960
for causing
numerous adverse reactions,

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.960
including asthma, headache,
and neuropsychiatric disorders.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.800
But is this so-called
monosodium glutamate "allergy"

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.920
a menace or a myth?

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.880
You don't know
until you put it to the test,

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.720
or in this case, many tests.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.760
In a multicenter, double-blind,
placebo-controlled,

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.440
multiple-challenge trial of MSG,

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.560
130 self-reported
MSG-reactive volunteers

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.160
first consumed beverages
containing 5 grams of MSG

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.320
or a placebo,

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.400
and those who reported
two or more

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.840
of 10 different symptoms,
such as flushing or headache,

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:55.280
were then rechallenged
in a dose-response study.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.760
Of the 19 people
who consistently responded

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.880
to 5 grams of MSG
mixed in a drink,

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.360
once MSG was given in a capsule

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.480
(therefore ensuring people

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.040
couldn't actually taste
the MSG,

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.520
which they could
when it was mixed in a drink),

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.000
only two people had reactions.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.760
The researchers noted that
large doses of MSG without food

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.320
may result in more symptoms,

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:22.400
but no persistent or serious
effects were observed.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.920
For context,
estimated MSG intakes in the UK

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.480
are about 0.58 grams per day,

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:29.760
and in Asian countries,

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.320
such as China, Korea,
and Vietnam,

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.320
where it is
a more commonly used seasoning,

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.880
it ranges from 0.33 grams
per day

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:39.440
to 3.8 grams per day,

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.360
which is generally much less
than the massive 5g or greater

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.640
single doses provided
in the challenge trials.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.680
Yet, only 2 out of 130 people

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.480
who claimed
to be sensitive to MSG

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:56.400
had any more symptoms than
when they just took a placebo.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.160
Given the controversy,

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.320
consensus meetings
with scientific experts

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:04.240
convened in 1997
and again in 2007,

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.800
and concluded that
MSG was harmless,

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.480
and no dietary intake limits
needed to be set.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:14.356
But, in 2017, the
European Food Safety Authority

00:03:14.356 --> 00:03:17.760
reevaluated the safety
of glutamate as a food additive,

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.240
and decided
to set a limit of 30 mg

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:23.120
per kilogram of body weight
per day,

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:26.240
which is exceeded by
most people every day.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.600
This limit was based off
a rat study from 1979,

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.600
with EFSA citing potential

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.280
neurodevelopmental
toxicity concerns.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.520
But even the author
of the original study

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.040
says they failed to convincingly
show such an effect.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.240
And given MSG's
salt-reducing benefits,

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:47.200
might it be a-okay
as a much-needed tool

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:50.080
to reduce sodium in foods?

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.480
Now, MSG still has sodium;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.160
it's monosodium glutamate,
after all;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:59.760
with about one third the sodium
of typical table salt.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.280
So, a better salt substitute
would be potassium salt,

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:04.560
potassium chloride,

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:08.083
which has the potential
to save even more lives.

